{"name":"Grasshopper","hex":"#77824a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grasshopper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mostly herbivorous insect of the order Orthoptera, noted for its ability to jump long distances and for the habit of some species communicating by stridulation; they are related to but distinct from crickets.","In the strict sense, refers to insects in the suborder Caelifera, particularly those in the family Acrididae."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of a crop, to be ruined or destroyed by grasshoppers.","To move a riverboat over an obstruction, such as a sandbar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grasshopper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grasshopper","blocks":[{"text":"Grasshoppers are a group of insects belonging to the suborder Caelifera. They are amongst what are possibly the most ancient living groups of chewing herbivorous insects, dating back to the early Triassic, around 250 million years ago."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/American_Bird_Grasshopper.jpg/330px-American_Bird_Grasshopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grasshopper","wikidata":"Q18398431"},{"title":"Grasshopper (cocktail)","blocks":[{"text":"A grasshopper is a sweet, mint-flavored, after-dinner alcoholic drink named for its green color, which comes from crème de menthe. Tujague's, a bar in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, claims its owner Philip Guichet invented the drink in 1918. The drink gained popularity during the 1950s and 1960s throughout the American South."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Grasshopper_cocktail.jpg/330px-Grasshopper_cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grasshopper_(cocktail)","wikidata":"Q1203995"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons